# systemd — real-world examples

## Analyze boot performance

```sh
# Total startup time
systemd-analyze

# Time each service cost, worst first
systemd-analyze blame

# Critical path of the boot
systemd-analyze critical-chain
```

## Manage units

```sh
systemctl list-units
sudo systemctl enable --now myservice.service
sudo systemctl restart myservice.service
```

## Where unit files live

```
/etc/systemd/system     # your units (highest priority)
/usr/lib/systemd/system  # packaged units
```

## Timer example — nightly web backup

```ini
# /etc/systemd/system/web-backup.service
[Unit]
Description=Back up the web root

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/sbin/web-backup.sh
```

```ini
# /etc/systemd/system/web-backup.timer
[Unit]
Description=Run the web backup nightly

[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* 23:00:00
Persistent=true
Unit=web-backup.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
```

```sh
# /usr/local/sbin/web-backup.sh
#!/bin/bash
DATE=$(date "+%d")
/usr/bin/tar -czf /srv/backups/site-backup-$DATE.tar.gz /srv/www/htdocs
```

## Enable the timer

```sh
sudo chmod 0755 /usr/local/sbin/web-backup.sh
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now web-backup.timer
systemctl status web-backup.timer
systemctl list-timers
```

> On Tumbleweed the default web root is `/srv/www/htdocs`. Point timers at
> `timers.target`, and use `journalctl -u web-backup.service` to read its logs.
